<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Simraungadh</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Simraongarh</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Simraungadh is a town and archaeological site in Bara District of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>, close to the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>. It was the capital of the Karnat dynasty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithila">Mithila</a> from the late 11th century until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghiyath-al-din-tughluq">Ghiyath al-Din Tughluq</a> took it in 1324. Remains of its walls, tanks and temples survive.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/simraungadh.jpg" alt="Simraungadh" width="320" height="153" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Scyfie · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jharokhar_Pokhari_Simroungarh.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Simraungadh lies on the flat, fertile plain of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/terai">Terai</a> at an average elevation of 83 metres, 90 kilometres south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu">Kathmandu</a> and 28 kilometres east of Birgunj, on the border with the Indian state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bihar">Bihar</a>. Its local language is Bhojpuri. The name is said to come from simr, the local word for the simal tree, and the Gopal Raj Vamshavali, the oldest chronicle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>, links the place with a simal forest. The Tibetan monk Dharmasvamin called it Pa-ta, read as a short form of pattana, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> for a capital.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Karnat dynasty, also called the Simraon or Dev dynasty, founded its kingdom here in 1097 CE and ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirhut">Tirhut</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithila">Mithila</a>, the land between the Mahananda, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a>, the Gandaki and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/himalayas">Himalayas</a>. The French indologist Sylvain Lévi thought that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanyadeva">Nanyadeva</a> established his supremacy over Simraungadh probably with the help of the Chalukya king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikramaditya-vi">Vikramaditya VI</a>. Karnat rule is remembered as a golden age of Tirhut, with the growth of local folk music and literature.</p><p>The city was enclosed by earthen ramparts and ditches, known locally as Baahi and remembered as a labyrinth, the main enclosure about 7.5 km from north to south and 4.5 km from east to west. Dharmasvamin, visiting in 1236 in the reign of Rama Singh Deva, described seven tall walls, a palace with eleven large gates, and 21 water-filled ditches lined with trees. After the fall of the Karnat dynasty the region passed to the Oiniwar or the Makwanpur rulers until the unification of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> in 1768. The Italian missionary Cassiano Beligatti, passing on 29 February 1740, saw old ruins inside high walls that could be entered at a single spot.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Simraungadh is a municipality of Bara District, formed in 2014 from several village development committees and later enlarged, and it holds part of an archaeological site divided between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. The Ranivas or Ram Janaki temple was built in 1878 by Jagat Jung Rana and is believed to stand on the Karnat palace; the Kankali Temple keeps a bell dated to 1283 and an idol believed to have been damaged by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tughlaq-dynasty">Tughlaq</a> armies, and the Jharokhar pond is thought to be about 800 years old.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Simraungadh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He made <b>Simraungadh</b>, near the modern India and Nepal border, his capital and ruled the region of Mithila between the Ganges and the Himalayan foothills.</blockquote><a href="/state/karnat-dynasty">Karnat dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
